codex
The false mutation built successfully and failed on the expected unmet result (`true` remained in `<k>` while the mutation demanded `false`), with the empty list confirmed as a satisfying witness. So the original claims are result-constraining, but Gate A still fails: the proof-only equality substitution is not a valid refinement of fixed execution. I’m consolidating the seven-stage findings, complete 945-item rule inventory, and trust ledger into `REVIEW.md`.
Fresh builds and all submitted positive claims succeeded, but validation exposed a Gate A failure: the priority-45 list-equality bridge is unconnected, globally under-covered, and causes concrete ground instances of the claimed theorem to get stuck. The fresh false-result mutation correctly failed.
  ✓ Read required Kit skills and check trusted-mode infrastructure boundary
  ✓ Stage 1: audit inputs, provenance, translator/prompt, and supplied-semantics integrity
  ✓ Stage 2: check program fidelity, regenerate MPython, and run independent differential tests
  ✓ Stage 3: reconstruct definitions from clean source and rerun all positive claims
  ✓ Stages 4–5: assess adequacy/real-program pinning and perform exhaustive static rule review
  ✓ Stage 6: build and run a fresh false non-vacuity mutation
  → Stage 7: account for proven versus assumed facts and write final REVIEW.md with verdict
